WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.167
The canonical papers.

2
00:00:02.467 --> 00:00:04.484
Top minds read primary sources.

3
00:00:04.484 --> 00:00:07.933
And you only need about thirty papers, read properly.

4
00:00:09.133 --> 00:00:10.200
Here is the pace.

5
00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:11.667
One paper a week.

6
00:00:11.967 --> 00:00:13.233
Seven months of that.

7
00:00:13.533 --> 00:00:17.200
Puts you ahead of about ninety five percent of applied engineers.

8
00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:20.400
The thirty papers come in three tiers.

9
00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:22.363
Tier one is the foundations.

10
00:00:22.363 --> 00:00:23.967
Read these first, in order.

11
00:00:24.267 --> 00:00:28.267
Tier two is the applied core, the ideas behind your daily work.

12
00:00:28.567 --> 00:00:32.367
Tier three is the current frontier, from twenty twenty four onward.

13
00:00:33.567 --> 00:00:35.167
Tier one has six papers.

14
00:00:35.467 --> 00:00:40.200
Attention Is All You Need: read it until the Q K V figure is obvious.

15
00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:43.500
BERT made pretraining plus fine-tuning a paradigm.

16
00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:47.533
GPT-3 showed that scale gives few-shot learning.

17
00:00:47.833 --> 00:00:51.467
Chinchilla corrected scaling: most models were undertrained.

18
00:00:51.767 --> 00:00:55.600
InstructGPT is the recipe that made ChatGPT.

19
00:00:55.900 --> 00:00:59.033
And chain of thought, the cheapest big idea in the field.

20
00:01:00.233 --> 00:01:01.800
Now, how to read a paper.

21
00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:05.123
Pass one, five minutes: title, abstract, figures.

22
00:01:05.123 --> 00:01:06.233
What is the claim?

23
00:01:06.533 --> 00:01:08.589
Pass two, twenty minutes: the method.

24
00:01:08.589 --> 00:01:09.867
Could I implement this?

25
00:01:10.167 --> 00:01:16.867
Pass three, only if it matters: reproduce the idea, check the baselines, and ask what would make it not work.

26
00:01:18.067 --> 00:01:23.833
The common mistake: most people stop at pass one, and quote the paper as if they did pass three.

27
00:01:24.133 --> 00:01:28.133
Do the full third pass on just ten papers, and you are in rare company.

28
00:01:29.333 --> 00:01:30.170
Let's recap.

29
00:01:30.170 --> 00:01:33.100
Read primary sources, about thirty papers.

30
00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:35.900
Start with the Tier one foundations, in order.

31
00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:37.567
Read in three passes.

32
00:01:37.867 --> 00:01:41.478
And in pass three, reproduce the idea and try to break it.

33
00:01:41.478 --> 00:01:43.533
Next up: the from-scratch builds.
